<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xiongnu Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xiongnu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Xiongnu Empire was a confederation of nomadic peoples that dominated the eastern Eurasian steppe from the late third century BCE to the first century CE. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a> it united the steppe tribes and became a strong rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>, extracting tribute through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a> system. Weakened by Han campaigns and internal division, it fragmented, though its political model influenced later steppe empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire.jpg" alt="Xiongnu Empire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Xiongnu_Gold_Crown_-_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mongolian Plateau of central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> was earlier inhabited by the Slab Grave Culture, which lasted until the 3rd century BCE. The language of the Xiongnu is disputed, and Turkic, Iranian, Mongolic and Yeniseian origins have all been proposed.</p><p>Their first known leader, Touman, reigned between 220 and 209 BCE. In 215 BCE Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> sent General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-tian">Meng Tian</a> against the Xiongnu, who drove them from the Ordos Loop and forced them to flee north into the Mongolian Plateau. In 209 BCE Touman's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the winter of 200 BCE, after a Xiongnu siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, Emperor Gaozu of Han led a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a> and was ambushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baideng">Battle of Baideng</a>, where he was cut off from supplies for seven days and narrowly escaped capture. Later the Han emperor Wu sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> west to seek an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> against the Xiongnu. The mission failed, but his reports prompted Han preparations to break the Xiongnu hold on the westward routes.</p><p>When the chanyu Xulüquanqu died in 60 BCE, a succession dispute began that lasted until 53 BCE. In that year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a> moved south and submitted to the Han, entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a> in which he was to pay homage, send a hostage prince and present tribute. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhizhi">Zhizhi</a> moved west and was killed by a Chinese army in 36 BCE near Lake Balkhash. A second succession quarrel around 47 CE left the Xiongnu divided into southern and northern groups. Those who resettled within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> gradually intermixed with the Han, and by the 7th century the Xiongnu had completely integrated into Chinese society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The ruler was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>, and below him stood the tuqi kings, of whom the Tuqi King of the Left was normally the heir. Beneath them were officials in left and right pairs and commanders of detachments of a thousand, a hundred and ten men, so that the nation was organised like an army. A weakness was the custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateral-succession">lateral succession</a>, in which a dead ruler's brother took power if his son was too young. The Xiongnu herded flocks and traded horses with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and ate mostly mutton, horse meat and wild geese. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> they called Heaven Chēnglí, a transcription of Tengri.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xiongnu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pushed west by the <b>Xiongnu</b> and the Wusun, the Yuezhi reached the land north of the Oxus, and around 140 BC the Saka began to invade Parthia and Bactria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han military combined a conscript infantry base with a professional cavalry arm developed specifically to counter the <b>Xiongnu</b> nomadic confederation, the empire's most persistent strategic threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the 18th century historians have debated a link with the <b>Xiongnu</b> of Mongolia, and there is no consensus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kushans were one of five branches of the Yuezhi, a people driven from the Gansu corridor by the <b>Xiongnu</b> in the 2nd century BCE, who settled in Bactria, north of the Hindu Kush, by about 130 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty allied with them against the <b>Xiongnu</b> state of Han-Zhao, and in 315 the chief Tuoba Yilu was made Prince of Dai; Dai stagnated after him and fell to Former Qin in 376.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 215 BCE the general Meng Tian conquered the Ordos from the <b>Xiongnu</b>, who had raided Qin peasants forbidden to trade with them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
